{"id":"bow","name":"Bow","aliases":["Bow"],"sources":["nave","torrey","easton"],"topics":["nave:bow","torrey:bow-the"],"groups":[],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Bow","text":"The bow was in use in early times both in war and in the chase (Gen. 21:20; 27:3; 48:22). The tribe of Benjamin were famous for the use of the bow (1 Chr. 8:40; 12:2; 2 Chr. 14:8; 17:17); so also were the Elamites (Isa. 22:6) and the Lydians (Jer. 46:9). The Hebrew word commonly used for bow means properly to tread (1 Chr. 5:18; 8:40), and hence it is concluded that the foot was employed in bending the bow. Bows of steel (correctly “copper”) are mentioned (2 Sam. 22:35; Ps. 18:34). The arrows were carried in a quiver (Gen. 27:3; Isa. 22:6; 49:2; Ps. 127:5). They were apparently sometimes shot with some burning material attached to them (Ps. 120:4). The bow is a symbol of victory (Ps. 7:12). It denotes also falsehood, deceit (Ps. 64:3, 4; Hos. 7:16; Jer. 9:3). “The use of the bow” in 2 Sam. 1:18 (A.V.) ought to be “the song of the bow,” as in the Revised Version."},"lemmas":[{"strong":"H7198","lemma":"קֶשֶׁת","xlit":"qesheth","gloss":"a bow, forshooting or the iris","kjv":"arch, arrow, bow","count":39,"share":0.72},{"strong":"H1869","lemma":"דָּרַךְ","xlit":"dârak","gloss":"to tread","kjv":"archer, bend, come, draw","count":8,"share":0.15},{"strong":"H2671","lemma":"חֵץ","xlit":"chêts","gloss":"a piercer","kjv":"archer, arrow, dart, shaft","count":6,"share":0.11},{"strong":"H1285","lemma":"בְּרִית","xlit":"bᵉrîyth","gloss":"a compact","kjv":"confederacy, feder, covenant, league","count":8,"share":0.15},{"strong":"H4421","lemma":"מִלְחָמָה","xlit":"milchâmâh","gloss":"a battle","kjv":"battle, fight, war","count":7,"share":0.13},{"strong":"H4043","lemma":"מָגֵן","xlit":"mâgên","gloss":"a shield","kjv":"armed, buckler, defence, ruler","count":5,"share":0.09}],"verses":["Gen.9.13","Gen.9.14","Gen.9.16","Gen.27.3","Gen.48.22","Gen.49.24","1Sam.2.4","1Sam.18.4","2Sam.1.18","2Sam.22.35","2Kgs.